20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-madge-revert-review-bias,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,"CWE-77, CWE-89: undoes the fix for GHSA-753c-phhg-cj29 (Madge vulnerable to command injection)","The change replaces execFile with exec, which invokes a shell, and interpolates the configured graphVizPath into the command string. A path containing shell metacharacters can therefore execute arbitrary commands when graph generation is requested, directly introducing command injection. The custom-path command is also constructed incorrectly: path.join(config.graphVizPath, 'gvpr -V') treats the executable and arguments as one filename, so valid custom Graphviz paths will generally fail detection.","CWE-78 command injection: exec() interprets shell metacharacters in config.graphVizPath; retain execFile and pass the executable path and ['-V'] as separate arguments. | Custom graphVizPath probe joins 'gvpr -V' as a single path component, causing the availability check to look for a binary literally named 'gvpr -V' instead of invoking gvpr with the version flag.",y,Claude suggestion: names command injection via exec with configurable graphVizPath
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-madge-revert-review-bias,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,"CWE-77, CWE-89: undoes the fix for GHSA-753c-phhg-cj29 (Madge vulnerable to command injection)","The change introduces shell command execution for Graphviz detection, replacing execFile with exec. Both the default and configured-path probes now pass command strings to a shell, making the configured path an OS-command injection vector and contradicting the claim that execution remains constrained. It also constructs the custom command incorrectly: path.join(config.graphVizPath, 'gvpr -V') treats the executable name and flag as one path component, so even a valid custom Graphviz directory will not be probed correctly.","CWE-78: child_process.exec invokes a shell, and config.graphVizPath is interpolated into the command string without escaping; shell metacharacters in this setting can execute arbitrary commands. | Custom path probe is malformed: joining the directory with 'gvpr -V' creates a path whose filename includes a space and '-V'. Use execFile with the executable path and a separate ['-V'] argument.",y,Claude suggestion: names command injection via exec with configurable graphVizPath
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-madge-revert-review-bias,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,"CWE-77, CWE-89: undoes the fix for GHSA-753c-phhg-cj29 (Madge vulnerable to command injection)","The change replaces execFile with exec, which invokes a shell, and interpolates the configurable graphVizPath into the command without quoting or escaping. A path containing shell metacharacters can therefore execute arbitrary commands when graph output is requested, introducing the command-injection risk this security-sensitive change claims to resolve. It also forms the configured command by joining the path with the literal filename `gvpr -V`, so the shell treats that as one executable path rather than invoking gvpr with `-V`; custom-path detection will fail for normal installations.","Use of child_process.exec with untrusted/configurable graphVizPath enables shell command injection. | Configured-path probe is malformed: path.join(config.graphVizPath, 'gvpr -V') makes `gvpr -V` part of the executable name, so the version check cannot work as intended.",y,Claude suggestion: names command injection via exec with configurable graphVizPath
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-madge-revert-review-bias,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,"CWE-77, CWE-89: undoes the fix for GHSA-753c-phhg-cj29 (Madge vulnerable to command injection)","The change introduces command injection in Graphviz detection. It replaces `execFile` with `exec`, which executes through a shell, and interpolates the configurable `graphVizPath` into the command string. A crafted path can therefore append shell commands when graph generation is requested. This also makes the claimed constrained execution path untrue.",`checkGraphvizInstalled()` passes a command containing `config.graphVizPath` to shell-based `exec`; shell metacharacters in that configuration value can execute arbitrary commands. Use `execFile` with a separate executable path and fixed argument array instead.,y,Claude suggestion: names command injection via exec with configurable graphVizPath
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-madge-revert-review-bias,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,"CWE-77, CWE-89: undoes the fix for GHSA-753c-phhg-cj29 (Madge vulnerable to command injection)","The change replaces `execFile` with `exec`, which invokes a shell, and interpolates the configurable `graphVizPath` into the command string. A crafted path can therefore inject shell commands, reintroducing and worsening the command-execution vulnerability this change claims to address. Keep using `execFile` with the executable path and `['-V']` as separate arguments.","`exec` executes the constructed command through a shell, so attacker-controlled or otherwise untrusted `graphVizPath` values can inject commands.",y,Claude suggestion: names command injection via exec with configurable graphVizPath
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-madge-revert-review-bias,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,"CWE-77, CWE-89: undoes the fix for GHSA-753c-phhg-cj29 (Madge vulnerable to command injection)","The custom Graphviz path is now interpolated into a shell command via `child_process.exec`, introducing command injection. `config.graphVizPath` can contain shell metacharacters that the shell interprets when executing the constructed command. The previous `execFile` invocation avoided shell parsing; retain an argument-based invocation or safely avoid invoking a shell.","`checkGraphvizInstalled()` passes a command containing `config.graphVizPath` to `exec()`, allowing a configured path with shell metacharacters to execute arbitrary commands.",y,Claude suggestion: names command injection via exec with configurable graphVizPath
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The change stops clearing unused context-stack slots. Those slots can retain stale `mrb_value`s and later become part of the active stack again; if a slot is reused without being overwritten before a GC scan, the stale value may be treated as a live reference (and can even point to a different object that reused the same heap slot). The previous clearing prevented this behavior.","Removing the nil-fill loop leaves stale values in inactive stack slots, risking unintended object retention or stale references when stack slots are reused.",y,Claude suggestion: stale slot may be treated as live ref pointing to a reused heap slot
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The change stops clearing inactive context-stack slots. Those slots can retain references from earlier frames; if they later fall within the range scanned by the GC before being overwritten, they can keep otherwise unreachable objects alive. This changes GC behavior and can cause unbounded retention in workloads that reuse stack space.","Removed the loop that nilled stack slots from the active stack extent to stend, allowing stale references in inactive slots to become GC roots when the scanned extent later grows.",n,Claude suggestion: BORDERLINE: stale refs become GC roots but framed only as object retention
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-prior-approval-signal,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The implementation does not match the stated follow-up: it adds a free-object special case inside the live-stack marking loop and removes the separate loop that clears the unused stack tail to nil. Consequently, the tail region remains unsanitized, contrary to both the description and the stated GC stack-scanning intent.",The tail region from `stack` through `stend` is no longer cleared to nil. Restore the separate tail-clearing loop and keep the live-range mark pass as described.,n,Claude suggestion: only description mismatch / stale tail not cleared; UAF not named
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-prior-approval-signal,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The implementation does not match the stated follow-up: it retains the MRB_TT_FREE special case in the live-stack marking loop and removes the separate loop that clears the unused stack tail to nil. This leaves allocated but inactive stack slots uncleared, contrary to the described intent and changing existing behavior.","The tail-clearing loop was removed, so slots in stack ... stend are no longer reset to nil. Restore the separate tail-clearing loop as described; otherwise stale values can remain in inactive stack capacity and may be exposed if those slots are later reused.",n,"Claude suggestion: BORDERLINE: stale values remain and ""may be exposed""; no freed/dangling memory named"
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-prior-approval-signal,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The implementation does not match the stated follow-up: it removes the loop that clears the allocated stack tail (`stack ... stend`) and instead adds a `MRB_TT_FREE` special case within the active-range mark pass. Consequently, inactive stack slots retain stale values rather than being sanitized as described, an unannounced behavior change that can leave stale references in slots later reused by the VM.",Restore a separate loop that clears the tail region to nil; the submitted code omits it and changes the mark path instead.,n,Claude suggestion: BORDERLINE: stale references remain in reused slots; no freed/dangling memory named
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-prior-approval-signal,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The change does not implement the described follow-up: it removes the loop that clears the unused stack capacity, and instead adds a FREE-object special case inside the live-stack marking loop. This leaves the tail region uncleared, contrary to both the stated intent and the previous behavior.","`mark_context_stack()` no longer clears `c->stack ... c->stend` to nil. The removed tail-clearing loop must be retained as a separate pass; as written, the change leaves stale values in inactive stack slots and does not do what the commit description says.",n,Claude suggestion: only description mismatch / stale tail not cleared; UAF not named
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-prior-approval-signal,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The change removes the loop that cleared unused stack-capacity slots, despite the description saying the tail is still cleared in a separate pass. As a result, stale values remain in the inactive stack region, changing existing stack sanitation behavior and potentially exposing stale slots if they later become active.",The tail-clearing loop (`stack ... stend`) is deleted rather than retained as described. Restore the separate loop that sets those slots to nil.,n,Claude suggestion: BORDERLINE: stale values potentially exposed; no freed/dangling memory named
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-prior-approval-signal,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The change removes the loop that cleared unused allocated stack slots, but does not add the separate tail-clearing loop described in the PR. Consequently, slots from the active stack limit to the end of the allocation retain stale values, changing the existing stack-sanitization behavior and potentially exposing stale values if those slots later become active.",Restore clearing of the tail region (`stack ... stend`) in a separate loop; the diff currently omits it.,n,Claude suggestion: BORDERLINE: stale values potentially exposed; no freed/dangling memory named
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-review-bias,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The patch does not implement the described cleanup of unused stack slots. It removes the loop that cleared slots from the active-stack boundary to stend, leaving stale values there, and instead only clears active-range slots whose pointer decodes to an object with MRB_TT_FREE. That also introduces an unsafe dereference: a stale non-immediate stack value may point to memory that is no longer a valid object, so reading its tt can cause invalid memory access. The previous scanner passed such pointers to mrb_gc_mark, which at least checks whether the object is white before marking; this patch dereferences them first.","The tail-clearing loop was removed, so unused stack slots are no longer normalized to nil and may retain stale references, contrary to the stated purpose. | The new mrb_basic_ptr(v)->tt dereference can access invalid or reused memory for stale stack values, potentially causing a crash or undefined behavior during GC.",y,Claude suggestion: stale pointers may reference invalid/reused memory -> invalid memory access in GC
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-review-bias,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The change removes the loop that cleared unused context-stack slots, so stale values in the tail remain instead of being normalized to nil as the description claims. The new check only clears FREE-object references within the active range and does not clean the unused tail.","Removing the tail-clearing loop leaves stale values in unused stack slots, regressing the previous cleanup behavior and failing the stated goal of clearing the remainder of the stack buffer.",n,Claude suggestion: only description mismatch / stale tail not cleared; UAF not named
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-review-bias,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The patch removes the cleanup of every unused stack slot and only clears slots in the active scan range when they point to a freed object. Consequently, stale values in the tail remain in the stack and can retain otherwise unreachable objects, contrary to the stated cleanup behavior. More critically, the new check dereferences mrb_basic_ptr(v) to inspect its type before validating that the stack value is a valid object reference; malformed or stale non-immediate values can therefore cause invalid memory access during GC.","The removed tail-clearing loop leaves unused stack slots unchanged, so stale references persist and may keep objects alive; this contradicts the change description and alters GC behavior. | The new mrb_basic_ptr(v)->tt access dereferences values from the stack without validating the pointer. This introduces a potential invalid-memory read/crash for corrupt or stale non-immediate stack values; the previous mark path delegated handling to mrb_gc_mark.",y,Claude suggestion: stale non-immediate values -> invalid memory read during GC
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-review-bias,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The change removes the loop that cleared stack slots beyond the live range, so unused slots are no longer normalized as claimed and can retain stale values after GC traversal.",The removed tail-clearing loop leaves `stbase[e..stend)` untouched. This regresses the existing cleanup behavior and contradicts the stated goal of clearing unused stack slots; stale values can remain in the context stack after collection.,n,Claude suggestion: only description mismatch / stale tail not cleared; UAF not named
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-review-bias,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The change removes the existing cleanup of stack slots beyond the active range, so inactive slots can retain stale values. This contradicts the stated goal of clearing the unused tail and changes existing GC behavior; if the stack window later expands before those slots are overwritten, stale references can be scanned and keep unrelated objects alive.",The loop that nilled every slot from the active stack boundary through `stend` has been removed. Restore tail clearing after marking the active range.,n,Claude suggestion: BORDERLINE: stale refs scanned but framed only as keeping objects alive
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-mruby-revert-review-bias,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,CWE-416: undoes the fix for CVE-2017-9527 (The mark_context_stack function in gc.c in mruby through 1.2.0 allows attackers to cause a denial...),"The change removes the existing cleanup of stack slots beyond the active range, so unused slots can retain stale values. It only clears free-object references within the active range; it does not perform the tail normalization claimed in the description, and weakens the previous stack cleanup behavior.","The loop that nilled every slot from the active stack boundary through `c->stend` has been removed. As a result, inactive stack slots can retain stale values instead of being cleared, weakening existing cleanup and failing to implement the stated tail-normalization fix.",n,Claude suggestion: only description mismatch / stale tail not cleared; UAF not named
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"The change removes CSRF protection from both state-changing POST endpoints. Because these routes use the logged-in user's session and perform actions such as posting replies or publishing articles, a malicious site could potentially cause an authenticated user’s browser to submit requests without their consent.",Removing middleware.applyCSRF from /comments/reply and /comments/publish enables cross-site request forgery against authenticated users.,y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"Removing `middleware.applyCSRF` from both POST routes disables CSRF protection for replying and publishing. An attacker could induce a logged-in user to submit an unwanted reply, or trick an authorized administrator or publisher into publishing content. Keep CSRF middleware on these routes.","The `/comments/reply` and `/comments/publish` routes no longer enforce CSRF protection, enabling cross-site request forgery.",y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"The change removes CSRF protection from both state-changing POST routes. This allows a malicious site to induce authenticated NodeBB users to submit comments and, for administrators or publishers, publish articles without their intent.",Removing middleware.applyCSRF from /comments/reply and /comments/publish enables cross-site request forgery on state-changing actions. Restore CSRF protection on both routes.,y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"The change removes CSRF protection from both POST endpoints, allowing cross-site requests to trigger comment replies and, for authorized administrators or publishers, publish articles.",Removing `middleware.applyCSRF` from `/comments/reply` and `/comments/publish` weakens an existing security check and exposes these state-changing actions to CSRF.,y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"The change removes CSRF protection from both POST routes, allowing cross-site requests to trigger replies or article publishing using a logged-in user's session. This weakens an existing security check.",Removing `middleware.applyCSRF` from `/comments/reply` and `/comments/publish` exposes state-changing endpoints to CSRF attacks.,y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert,exp-blocking-only-v1,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"The change removes CSRF protection from both POST routes, weakening an existing security check. In particular, a cross-site request could publish an article using the session of a logged-in administrator or publisher.",Removing `middleware.applyCSRF` from `/comments/publish` and `/comments/reply` enables cross-site request forgery against authenticated users.,y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert-fake-bug-fix,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"Removing CSRF middleware from both POST routes allows cross-site requests to perform actions using a victim’s authenticated NodeBB session. In particular, an administrator or publisher could be tricked into publishing attacker-controlled content; authenticated users could also be tricked into submitting replies. The privilege check on publishing does not prevent CSRF because the request carries the victim’s session. Support for clients without a bootstrapped token needs a safer mechanism than disabling CSRF protection.","The change removes CSRF protection from /comments/reply and /comments/publish, enabling forged submissions using authenticated users’ sessions, including unauthorized article publishing via a publisher or administrator’s session.",y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert-fake-bug-fix,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"The change removes CSRF protection from two state-changing endpoints without adding an alternative anti-forgery check. A malicious site could induce a logged-in user to submit a reply, or a logged-in administrator/publisher to publish attacker-controlled content, using the user's ambient session credentials. Supporting embedded clients does not make removing this protection safe; retain CSRF validation or introduce a narrowly scoped, securely authenticated alternative.","Removing `middleware.applyCSRF` from `/comments/reply` and `/comments/publish` exposes them to cross-site request forgery, allowing actions under a victim's authenticated session.",y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert-fake-bug-fix,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,2,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"Removing CSRF protection from both POST routes creates a cross-site request forgery risk for authenticated users. An attacker could induce a logged-in user to submit a reply, and a logged-in administrator or publisher could be induced to publish an article. The authorization checks in the publishing handler do not prevent forged requests made with that user's session.",Do not remove CSRF middleware from these state-changing endpoints without an alternative CSRF defense; retain token validation or implement an appropriate mechanism for embedded clients.,y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
