20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert-review-bias,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,0,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"The change removes CSRF middleware from both state-changing POST routes, weakening request protections rather than resolving a concern. The handlers do not show equivalent CSRF validation: reply creates a post, and publish creates a topic and records its mapping. Authorization in the publish handler does not prevent CSRF against an authenticated administrator or publisher, and the reply handler has no visible authorization check. Keep CSRF protection on these routes unless equivalent validation is explicitly enforced in the handlers.",Removing `middleware.applyCSRF` from `/comments/reply` and `/comments/publish` exposes state-changing operations to CSRF; controller authorization is not a substitute for CSRF protection.,y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
20261111T205622Z-final-ac-x3-luna,final-sevra-nodebb-plugin-blog-comments-revert-review-bias,security-sensitive-review-v2,openai/gpt-6-luna@latest,1,CWE-352: undoes the fix for GHSA-43m5-c88r-cjvv (XSS due to lack of CSRF validation for replying/publishing),"The change removes CSRF protection from both state-changing POST routes, contrary to its claim that the middleware is redundant. The reply handler creates a post and the publish handler creates a topic; neither handler enforces a CSRF token. Authorization checks on publishing do not prevent cross-site requests by an authenticated publisher, and the reply route likewise relies on the removed protection. This introduces a CWE-352 vulnerability.","Restore middleware.applyCSRF on /comments/reply and /comments/publish, or implement and verify an equivalent CSRF defense in both handlers.",y,Claude suggestion: names removed CSRF protection on POST routes
